  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nCyclonic flow is found across the northern Plains this \nafternoon, with clearing skies as high pressure builds in over \nwestern North Dakota this afternoon. With the surface pressure \ngradient tightening between this approaching surface high and an\nexiting surface low to our east, breezy south winds sustained \naround 15 to 20 MPH and gusting as high as 30 MPH have \ndeveloped. Breezy winds are expected to linger overnight as we \nremain fairly well mixed and the surface gradient is slow to \nrelax. High temperatures this afternoon are forecast from the \nmid 40s in the James River Valley, up to the lower to mid 50s \nwest. Dry conditions are expected across North Dakota, \nespecially in the southwest where relative humidity values are \nexpected to drop into the 20 percent range. With the gusty south\nwinds this afternoon, near critical fire weather conditions \nwill persist through the early evening. Chilly overnight \nconditions are expected again with the clear skies over the \nforecast area, though the sustained winds are expected to \nprevent another widespread freeze like we saw last night.\n\nA building upper level ridge across the Great Plains this \nweekend will promote mild and dry weather across much of western\nand central North Dakota. Gusty south to southwest winds \nsustained around 15 MPH and gusts up to 30 MPH will persist over\nmuch of the forecast area through Saturday afternoon, though \nare anticipated to rapidly diminish through the early evening. \nWith the warm forecast highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s Saturday \nis expected to be even more dry than Friday, with minimum \nrelative humidity dropping as low as 15 to 20 percent across \nmuch of the southwest. With the lower winds on Saturday, sub \nnear critical fire weather conditions are currently expected, \nthough we will continue to monitor forecast trends over the next\nfew days.\n\nThe upper level ridge is then expected to begin flattening \nthrough the day on Sunday as a northern stream waves passes \nthrough the Canadian Prairies. While highs in the 60s are still \nanticipated in the afternoon, clearing skies and very light \nwinds could allow for another widespread freeze across much of \nthe area Sunday night through Monday morning. Current ensemble \nprobabilities of dropping to or below freezing is around 20 to \n40 percent across much of the north and portions of the \nsouthwest Monday morning. \n\nWith zonal flow then favored through the first half of the \ncoming week, mild and dry weather is anticipated to continue \nthrough at least the mid week. Through this period, a Pacific \nLow is expected to make landfall in the southwestern CONUS \nbefore cutting through the Rockies up toward the northern \nPlains. Some variation in timing and relative strength of this \nupper level feature is found within the ensemble, but overall \nchances for precipitation is anticipated to increase starting \nFriday and into the weekend.",
